# Invention No. 10 in G major, BWV 781 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a gigue in 9/8, three beats to the bar, almost entirely
# built from broken chords. The subject is a bar of leaping G major
# arpeggio in eighths, and a bar that starts with a trilled F-sharp; the
# bass answers it on D. Lively and dancing, but the arpeggios need room
# to ring, so not a race.
#
# Time signature: 9/8, 32 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI;
# the first note is on the downbeat of bar 1.
#
# Tempo: dotted quarter = 96 (eighth = 288), 96 beats, about 1:00.
# Track times (approximate): Gustav Leonhardt (harpsichord, Apple Music
# reissue) 1:00, about 96; Andras Schiff (ECM, clavichord) 0:59, about
# 98; Angela Hewitt (Hyperion) 0:46, about 125; Glenn Gould (Sony, 1964)
# 0:40, about 144, famously fast. 96 follows Leonhardt and Schiff: a
# natural gigue that still lets the arpeggios sound on the chip.
#
# Structure (read from the Mutopia score and checked against the MIDI;
# the arpeggio subject and the sequence on a figure that changes harmony
# every bar agree with the Japanese Piano Encyclopedia (PTNA) entry; the
# bar-by-bar plan below is my own reading, no published bar-by-bar
# analysis was found):
#   1-2    Subject, top, G major (bass silent in bar 1).
#   2-3    Subject, bottom, on D; top has the countersubject.
#   4-6    Imitative sequence, falling by step (A minor, G, D).
#   7-13   Sequence in long trilled notes against eighths, modulating
#          to D major; cadence in D major at 13-14.
#   14     Subject, bottom, D major (top silent after its D).
#   15     Subject, top, on A.
#   16     Subject, bottom, on D, with C natural: toward G.
#   17     Subject, top, G, turning toward C and E minor.
#   18-19  Sequence on trilled bass notes.
#   20-23  Long trilled C (20-21) and B (22-23) held in the top while the
#          bass arpeggiates the subject's head through a cycle of
#          fifths (A minor, D, G, E minor).
#   24-26  A long C held in the bass under falling top scales, then
#          D7 over C (26): the dominant prepares the return.
#   27-28  Subject, top, G major, as at the start (bass with it in a
#          varied arpeggio), then on D (28).
#   29-32  Closing: a cadence formula stated twice (29-30, 31-32),
#          final cadence in G major.

tempo 96 4.          # dotted quarter, three beats to the 9/8 bar

voice mid 0          # two voices only: there is no middle voice to set back

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-13: the first section, G major to D major
lead 2-3 bottom      # answer on D in the bass (bar 1 is the top alone)

dyn 14 mp            # bars 14-19: after the D major cadence the entries
                     # pass between the hands, one bar each: a softer manual
lead 15 top          # subject, top, on A (bar 14 and 16 the bass has it;
lead 16 bottom       # bar 14 the bass is alone, so no mark needed there)
lead 17 top          # subject, top, G

dyn 20 mf            # bars 20-26: the circle of fifths under the held
                     # notes, and the C pedal
lead 20-23 bottom    # the bass carries the subject's head under the
                     # held top notes
broaden 26 0.93      # bar 26: a breath on D7 before the return

dyn 27 f             # bars 27-32: the return in G major and the close
lead 27-28 top       # subject, top, G major then on D

rit 1.5 0.75         # from the middle of bar 31 into the final G
